    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-10-2467-2010",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Large cloud condensation nuclei (CCN) (e.g., aged dust particles and seasalt) cannot attain their equilibrium size during the typical timescale of cloud droplet activation. Cloud activation parameterizations applied to aerosol with a large fraction of large CCN often do not account for this limitation adequately and can give biased predictions of cloud droplet number concentration (CDNC). Here we present a simple approach to address this problem that can easily be incorporated into cloud activation parameterizations. This method is demonstrated with activation parameterizations based on the \"population splitting\" concept of Nenes and Seinfeld (2003); it is shown that accounting for large CCN effects eliminates a positive bias in CDNC where the aerosol dry geometric diameter is greater than 0.5 \u03bcm. The method proposed here can also be extended to include the water vapor depletion from pre-existing droplets and ice crystals in global and regional atmospheric models.",
    "field": "Aerosol-cloud interactions",
    "problem": "Cloud activation parameterizations often give biased predictions of cloud droplet numbers because they fail to account for the kinetic limitation that prevents large CCN (e.g., dust, sea-salt) from reaching their equilibrium size within the typical timescale of cloud droplet activation.",
    "method": "The authors propose a method to correct cloud activation parameterizations by adding a term to the water vapor balance equation that accounts for condensation onto large, kinetically-limited cloud condensation nuclei that do not reach their equilibrium size on the timescale of activation.",
    "task": "The task is to develop and demonstrate a simple method to correct cloud activation parameterizations for the kinetic growth limitations of giant cloud condensation nuclei (CCN), which cause biased predictions of cloud droplet number concentration.",
